WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.600
With fewer content restrictions than an MCU&nbsp;
film and more room to flesh out its characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.200
one of the best parts of Marvel's Netflix series&nbsp;
was its villains. From ninjas to mercenaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.120
from maniacs to crime lords, here are the MCU's&nbsp;
Netflix villains ranked from worst to best.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.720
The Hand is a criminal organization whose&nbsp;
shuriken-tossing soldiers have been leaping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.760
through Marvel's comics for a long time,&nbsp;
and they make their presence felt in half of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.480
Marvel's Netflix offerings. While making them&nbsp;
an integral part of Charlie Cox’s Daredevil's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.320
story is accurate to the source material, you&nbsp;
could argue that in the MCU Netflix series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.440
they were a big part of the&nbsp;
series' drop in quality.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.320
The Netflix heroes were best when dealing&nbsp;
with challenges grounded in the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.360
Daredevil's greatest villain is a crime lord.&nbsp;
Krysten Ritter's Jessica Jones wrestles with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.640
trauma she endured from a sexual predator. And Jon&nbsp;
Bernthal's Frank Castle goes up against corrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.400
soldiers for hire in The Punisher's first season.&nbsp;
These are all exaggerated versions of people who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.080
sadly, exist in the real world. Making a&nbsp;
centuries-old clandestine organization filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.600
with ninjas and superpowered warriors brought the&nbsp;
narrative to a place that felt more like fantasy.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:14.960
“Yeah, a little bit.”

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.040
When it comes to bad guys, you can't go wrong&nbsp;
with Clancy Brown. The man who famously played the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.280
merciless Kurgen in 1986's Highlander shows up in&nbsp;
season 2 of Daredevil as Colonel Ray Schoonover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.040
He is one of the men responsible for&nbsp;
the death of Frank Castle's family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.720
As one of the leaders of the Cerberus&nbsp;
Squad, Schoonover secretly funneled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.640
heroin from Afghanistan to the United&nbsp;
States in order to line his pockets.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.040
The colonel's low placement on the list&nbsp;
is only because we hardly get to know him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.440
Schoonover shows up for only two episodes&nbsp;
of Daredevil, and in his second appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.880
he becomes one of Frank Castle's victims. Brown&nbsp;
briefly reprises the role for flashbacks in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.920
first season of The Punisher. He's great at&nbsp;
conveying the feel of a once proud officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.320
who's now a slave to his need for&nbsp;
power and wealth. But unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.320
we only get so much time with him before&nbsp;
Castle gives him what he deserves.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:14.080
Willis Stryker, aka Diamondback, is played by Erik&nbsp;
LaRay Harvey, and he starts off strong. He first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.880
appears halfway through Luke Cage's inaugural&nbsp;
season, and he quickly becomes the biggest threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:24.000
to the titular hero. It's Stryker who brings the&nbsp;
Hammer Industries tech into Harlem to fight Luke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.920
Cage. This includes the Judas Bullets — the only&nbsp;
ammo capable of piercing Cage's skin — and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.520
suit of body armor he wears to fight Cage in the&nbsp;
season 1 finale. Of all antagonists in that first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.320
season, he's the only one who presents&nbsp;
any kind of physical challenge to Cage.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.080
But in part, it's the physical threat to Cage&nbsp;
that ruins Diamondback. His Iron Man-light suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.200
is just about the goofiest looking thing in&nbsp;
the entire Luke Cage series. It contrasts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.760
sharply against a show otherwise set in&nbsp;
a world that doesn't feel too far removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.760
from the real one. Their big brawl at the&nbsp;
end of the first season, while engaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.840
can take you out of the story faster than&nbsp;
just about anything else in the series.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.520
Sowande, played by Babs Olusanmokun, is&nbsp;
one of the founders of the Hand. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.120
one of the so-called Five Fingers of the Hand,&nbsp;
Sowande has had his life extended for centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.880
and he hasn't been idle in that time. Sowande is&nbsp;
a master of martial arts. Without any superhuman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.040
strength backing him up, he's able to make short&nbsp;
work of Luke Cage through the manipulation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.920
pressure points in The Defenders, and&nbsp;
he's likewise undefeated by Iron Fist.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.840
But despite his martial arts prowess,&nbsp;
Sowande is one of the more forgettable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.120
villains of MCU's Netflix series. He lives and&nbsp;
dies within the only season of The Defenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.480
beheaded by Daredevil's mentor&nbsp;
Stick, played by Scott Glenn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.760
and denied the immortal life that he and the&nbsp;
other leaders of the Hand crave. If most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:54.240
The Defenders is best left forgotten — and it is&nbsp;
— then Sowande has a head start. Pun intended.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.800
Elodie Yung's portrayal of Elektra&nbsp;
Natchios in season 2 of Daredevil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.640
is one of the bright spots in the&nbsp;
weakest of the show's three seasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.520
Yung perfectly embodies the deadly warrior&nbsp;
who's both in love with Matthew Murdock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.720
and wants him to adopt less merciful tactics in&nbsp;
his role as Daredevil. With her multiple deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.480
and resurrections in the source material, a tragic&nbsp;
death and a dark return were all but guaranteed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.840
Even so, it's after her resurrection in The&nbsp;
Defenders that this warrior loses her spark.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.240
When she's brought back to life in The Defenders,&nbsp;
Elektra may still be good at fighting and killing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:34.160
but she simply isn't as compelling a character.&nbsp;
As Alexandra Reid's right-hand woman, Elektra is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.920
practically mindless, not truly emerging from her&nbsp;
post-resurrection haze until she kills Reid. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.480
possible we could have seen a more interesting&nbsp;
version of Elektra as a villain if Daredevil had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.280
continued, but unfortunately, her final appearance&nbsp;
is in the series finale of The Defenders.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.600
The Bride of Nine Spiders, played by Jane Kim,&nbsp;
proves a worthy opponent for Danny Rand in season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:01.600
1 of Iron Fist. The seductive and skilled martial&nbsp;
artist comes close to defeating the hero, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.040
compared to the other villains on this list, the&nbsp;
Bride has a couple of things working against her.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:11.360
First, we never see the Bride again&nbsp;
after "Immortal Emerges from Cave,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.080
giving her a distinct "villain of the week"&nbsp;
feel. Second, while we normally wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:22.080
automatically fault a series from diverting from&nbsp;
the source material, the Bride is a special case.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.200
In the comics, we first meet the Bride in&nbsp;
2007's Immortal Iron Fist #8 as part of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.920
tournament with the champions of all the immortal&nbsp;
cities. When it's her turn to fight in the arena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.560
the Bride displays the impressive and&nbsp;
terrifying ability to summon seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.920
endless swarms of spiders at her opponents.&nbsp;
Adapting the character without that ability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.440
especially considering the incredible&nbsp;
visuals it could've led to, feels criminal.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:54.720
Theo Rossi has a knack for getting memorable roles&nbsp;
with unique nicknames. On Sons of Anarchy, Rossi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.600
played the biker Juice, while on Luke Cage, he&nbsp;
played one of the series' more interesting crooks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:05.040
— Shades. In the first season, Shades is fairly&nbsp;
low in the Stokes crime family pecking order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.160
but even then, there's a lot going on behind&nbsp;
the lenses of his namesake. While people like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:15.360
Cottonmouth, played by Mahershala Ali and Mariah&nbsp;
Dillard, played by Alfre Woodard, may be the ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.640
sitting on Harlem's throne at any given time, it's&nbsp;
Shades manipulating things behind the curtain.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:25.520
In the second season, Shades moves up the&nbsp;
ladder, owing in no small part to his romance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.800
with Dillard. His cool facade finally cracks&nbsp;
when Dillard forces him to kill his true love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.200
Comanche, played by Thomas Q. Jones. It&nbsp;
puts him on the path of betraying Dillard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.680
but he remains the same charismatic,&nbsp;
cool bad guy to the end of the series.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.680
Few characters seem to bounce back and forth&nbsp;
between friend and enemy as much as Will Simpson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.680
played by Wil Traval, does in the&nbsp;
first season of Jessica Jones. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.800
complex journey is as compelling as it&nbsp;
is tumultuous. Introduced as a policeman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.200
manipulated to attack Trish Walker by&nbsp;
the mind-bending abilities of Kilgrave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:03.600
Simpson tries to make things up to his victim&nbsp;
once he's free of Kilgrave's influence.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.960
Simpson eventually wins Walker's trust, but&nbsp;
his road to redemption takes dark turns. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.320
learn that in the military, Simpson was a part&nbsp;
of some scary experiments, including being given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.120
drugs to enhance his abilities, and he begins&nbsp;
taking those drugs once more. While they may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.800
help him physically, the drugs wreck his sanity,&nbsp;
putting him on a collision course with Jessica.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:30.320
Will reappears in season 2, but he doesn't last&nbsp;
long. Once again trying to make up for his crimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.760
Simpson tries to protect Trish, but&nbsp;
he's killed by Jessica's mother.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.840
In some ways, you could say Davos, played by&nbsp;
Sacha Dhawan, is to Danny Rand what the MCU's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:45.520
Loki is to Thor. Just as Loki is jealous of&nbsp;
the love and admiration all of Asgard feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.720
for the god of thunder, Davos envies Danny for&nbsp;
winning the mantle of Iron Fist. Even more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.640
Davos hates Danny for what he sees as squandering&nbsp;
that power. He's Danny's primary antagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:03.040
in season 2 of Iron Fist, going to increasingly&nbsp;
darker lengths to claim Rand's power for his own.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:05.360
Since Davos is introduced in the first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:09.200
we get to watch his descent from&nbsp;
Danny Rand's ally to his nemesis.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:14.240
“I’m not going to give up hope that,&nbsp;
one day, you’ll come back to yourself.”

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:20.720
Dhawan is believable as the villain known in the&nbsp;
comics as the Steel Serpent, and his conflict with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:26.320
Rand is one of the things that makes Iron Fist's&nbsp;
second season a vast improvement from the first.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:33.200
While the Hand pops up in both Daredevil and&nbsp;
Iron Fist, it's finally in The Defenders that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:39.200
meet its leader, Alexandra Reid. Sigourney Weaver&nbsp;
doesn't play villains often enough, and her time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:45.520
as Alexandra Reid is proof. Weaver is perfectly&nbsp;
chosen as the Hand's otherwise immortal leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:50.960
who finally feels the approach of death.&nbsp;
As Reid, she exudes power, ruthlessness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.840
and nothing more than perhaps some dismissive&nbsp;
pity for the band of heroes trying to stop her.

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.440
Sadly, while Weaver is one of the best reasons&nbsp;
to watch The Defenders, that list isn't very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.480
long. Among other flaws, the series was too&nbsp;
short and packed with too many characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.160
Before we hardly get a chance to&nbsp;
enjoy Weaver in this villainous role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:14.480
Elektra kills her. It's a shame that when&nbsp;
it was Weaver's time to join the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:17.520
it was in a role that didn't have&nbsp;
the time to stretch its legs.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.080
Sure, the first season of Iron Fist is&nbsp;
probably one of the weakest seasons of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.800
all of Marvel's Netflix series, but as a&nbsp;
bad guy, Harold Meachum is pretty great.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.040
The manipulative, solitary killer spends&nbsp;
much of the series in his penthouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.520
plotting and abusing his grown children.&nbsp;
Then there's the brokenness he brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.240
to Danny’s family, as he was the one who&nbsp;
arranged for the deaths of Danny's parents.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:46.000
David Wenham is great as Meachum, who delves&nbsp;
deeper into insanity as the season continues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.000
caught between Iron Fist and the&nbsp;
Hand. Wenham is likely best known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.080
Boromir's more resilient brother,&nbsp;
Faramir, in the Lord of the Rings films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:58.960
So, it's refreshingly unsettling to&nbsp;
see him in a role that pushes so hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:04.160
in the other direction. It's too bad the season&nbsp;
as a whole wasn't as good as Wenham's performance.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:09.760
Alisa Jones, played by Janet McTeer, never&nbsp;
really had much of a chance. In Jessica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:15.040
Jones' second season, we learn she survived&nbsp;
the car crash that killed her son and husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.760
after which she was nursed back to health&nbsp;
and given powers by the scientist Karl Malus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:25.440
By the time Alisa and Jessica are reunited,&nbsp;
Alisa's dedication to Malus reaches a manic pitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.720
making her dangerous to anyone near&nbsp;
her, especially after Malus dies.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:35.840
After the much more cerebral and emotional&nbsp;
threat of Kilgrave in Jessica Jones' first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:40.000
season, someone who could pose more of&nbsp;
a physical challenge to the hero was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.840
obvious choice. And McTeer&nbsp;
is wonderful in the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:48.000
She brings a fierceness to Alisa that&nbsp;
makes you believe she's capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:53.520
anything — including hurting, or even killing,&nbsp;
the daughter she's just been reunited with.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.440
The assassin John Pilgrim, played by Josh Stewart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:02.640
is singular in the ranks of Punisher villains.&nbsp;
This is for no other reason than the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.760
the guy actually survives his clash with&nbsp;
Frank Castle. With his priest's collar and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:12.880
the unsettling calm with which he doles&nbsp;
out violence, Pilgrim is one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:18.240
chilling and disturbing villains to show up in&nbsp;
The Punisher or any of Marvel's Netflix shows.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.360
We eventually learn that as&nbsp;
skilled as he is at killing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:26.240
Pilgrim is far from being a conscienceless&nbsp;
killer. A former member of the Aryan Brotherhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.320
Pilgrim eventually saw the evil of&nbsp;
his ways, converted to Christianity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:34.800
and became a pastor. His bloody rampage&nbsp;
through the second season of The Punisher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:39.760
is the result of the manipulative Schultzes&nbsp;
keeping his children hostage. It's Pilgrim's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.040
dedication to his family that convinces Frank&nbsp;
to spare his life in spite of his crimes.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:52.080
Trish Walker is perhaps the most tragic character&nbsp;
in any of Marvel's Netflix series. When we meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.640
her in the first season of Jessica Jones,&nbsp;
she's the titular hero's best friend and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:01.920
the survivor of a horrible assault. As the&nbsp;
series continues, her desire to be more like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.360
Jessica leads her to increasingly dangerous&nbsp;
extremes. Unfortunately, this includes her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:12.480
taking the same addicting medications that gave&nbsp;
the late Will Simpson his enhanced abilities.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:18.160
Surprised to find herself with powers, Walker goes&nbsp;
down roads she can't turn back from in the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:24.240
final season. She becomes a self-styled vigilante,&nbsp;
but her time as a crime-fighter leaves a lot to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:29.200
desired. Among other things, she accidentally&nbsp;
causes one attacker to fall on her own weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:34.640
and flees the scene rather than helping. She kills&nbsp;
Gregory Sallinger just before the series finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:39.280
and the final battle of Jessica Jones&nbsp;
proves to be between Jones and Walker.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:45.840
William Rawlins, played by Paul Schulze, is&nbsp;
precisely the kind of guy Frank Castle was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:50.720
born to kill. When Castle first meets&nbsp;
Rawlins in Afghanistan, the CIA agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.760
seems like someone Castle is&nbsp;
destined to lock horns with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:58.240
This is even before Castle learns that Rawlins&nbsp;
and Schoonover are using their squad to smuggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:02.880
heroin back to the United States. It's also&nbsp;
Rawlins who orders the hit on Castle's life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:07.760
leading to deaths of his wife and their two&nbsp;
children. So, inevitably, it is Rawlins who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:12.880
meets his own brutal end at Castle's hands&nbsp;
before The Punisher's first season is over.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:18.160
With his freezing glare and permanent&nbsp;
scowl, Paul Schulze is utterly believable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:23.120
as the corrupt operative who left his&nbsp;
conscience far behind long ago. He's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:28.720
perfect villain for The Punisher's inaugural&nbsp;
season, and his air of cold arrogance clashes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:34.240
wonderfully with the more likable villainy&nbsp;
of Frank Castle's old friend, Billy Russo.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:39.760
What do you say about a man who almost kills a&nbsp;
chef because he doesn't like how he cooks steak?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:44.240
Well, you call him whatever you want,&nbsp;
just make sure it's out of his earshot.

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:48.400
In the final season of Jessica Jones,&nbsp;
Jeremy Bobb plays Gregory Sallinger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:53.200
a serial killer who targets anyone he views&nbsp;
as being morally bankrupt or unworthy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:59.200
their place in society. Could be bad chefs,&nbsp;
blackmailers, or superpowered private detectives.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:04.240
Sallinger is highly intelligent and has spent&nbsp;
his life honing the different skills he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:09.680
need to be a prolific serial killer. But as&nbsp;
challenging an enemy as he is to Jessica Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:15.440
his crushing self-loathing and self-doubt shine&nbsp;
through more than anything else. It's clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:20.160
that no matter how much he accomplishes&nbsp;
or what he proves himself capable of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:23.520
the voices inside Sallinger will&nbsp;
never allow it to be enough.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:26.640
“And that is extraordinary.”

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:31.760
In the third season of Daredevil, Benjamin&nbsp;
Poindexter, played by Wilson Bethel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:37.360
is a FBI agent who Wilson Fisk manipulates&nbsp;
into becoming his worst self. Bethel plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:43.200
a man who's learned to manage his mental illness.&nbsp;
Fisk learns Poindexter's coping skills and works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:49.440
to tear them all down so that all Poindexter is&nbsp;
left with is madness. As a killer, Poindexter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:53.840
is arguably the greatest physical challenge&nbsp;
Murdock faces in the course of his series.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:58.000
Pointdexter’s backstory was an addition not&nbsp;
found in his comic character, where he goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:03.120
by “Bullseye.” He is never directly referred&nbsp;
to as "Bullseye" in the show. However, the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:08.560
of season 3 strongly hinted that we were going&nbsp;
to see the return of the former FBI agent. Were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:13.280
the series to have continued, maybe he would’ve&nbsp;
come back as “Bullseye.” Guess we’ll never know.

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:19.840
Few villains change as drastically between&nbsp;
seasons as The Punisher's Billy Russo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.120
played by Ben Barnes. In the beginning&nbsp;
of the first season of The Punisher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:27.680
Russo acts like Frank Castle's friend, but&nbsp;
we soon learn he was in bed with Rawlins and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:32.320
Schoonover in the Cerberus Squad. Of all of&nbsp;
Castle's enemies from his special ops days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:35.840
Russo is the only one to survive&nbsp;
the first season, but he doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:40.800
do it unscathed. Castle leaves Russo with&nbsp;
the scars that earn him the name Jigsaw.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.880
Traumatized by his confrontation&nbsp;
with Castle, Russo temporarily loses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:50.320
all memory of the last few years in The Punisher's&nbsp;
second season. Here, actor Ben Barnes shows us a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:56.160
more vulnerable side of Jigsaw, who spends months&nbsp;
recovering mentally and physically. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.160 --> 00:16:00.640
Billy Russo acts true to form, seducing&nbsp;
his therapist and building a new gang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:05.360
of crooks and killers. But Castle gives&nbsp;
Russo the death he deserves in the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:10.960
In his final moments, Russo apologizes to his&nbsp;
old friend, but Castle hardly seems to care.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:15.680
It isn't tough to imagine how&nbsp;
Cornell Stokes, aka Cottonmouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:19.600
won the loyalty of the crooks under his&nbsp;
command. Along with the more obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:24.240
threats and incentives someone like Stokes&nbsp;
would use to build a criminal organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:29.520
of all the villains we meet in Luke Cage,&nbsp;
he has the most magnetic personality.

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:34.320
Mahershala Ali's brilliant performance&nbsp;
is mesmerizing enough that you actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:39.520
believe the confidence of that big laugh of&nbsp;
his. It doesn’t matter that one confrontation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:45.360
after another shows he has no good ideas about&nbsp;
how to handle Luke Cage. His death at Mariah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:50.240
Dillard's hands in the middle of the first&nbsp;
season is that much more shocking because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:54.960
of Ali's work. Even as Mariah furiously&nbsp;
finishes him off with a mic stand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.960 --> 00:17:00.400
it's tough to believe the series is getting rid of&nbsp;
one of its most entertaining characters so soon.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:06.720
Bushmaster, played by Mustafa Shakir, is the first&nbsp;
villain in Luke Cage to give the titular hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:11.360
any kind of physical challenge without&nbsp;
the help of Judas Bullets or power suits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:16.560
In the opening moments of season 2's "I&nbsp;
Get Physical," Bushmaster leaves Luke Cage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:21.520
unconscious on the Harlem sidewalk using&nbsp;
only his martial arts skills, his speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:26.320
and his enhanced strength. The Brooklyn&nbsp;
native uses the Jamaican herb Nightshade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:31.600
to boost his strength and durability, rendering&nbsp;
his skin almost as impervious as Cage's.

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:37.200
Bushmaster is a clever, charismatic bad guy, and&nbsp;
it's tough to not sympathize with him. He's in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:43.040
Harlem looking for vengeance on the Stokeses. His&nbsp;
own family was killed by the late Stokes matriarch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:49.120
Mama Mabel and her gang. Cage and Bushmaster&nbsp;
eventually forge an uneasy alliance to stop Mariah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:54.320
Dillard, but it ends when Bushmaster proves more&nbsp;
willing to resort to murder than Cage would like.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:00.240
Two episodes before Wilson Fisk appears in&nbsp;
Daredevil's first season, we're introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:05.840
to his most trusted adviser — James Wesley,&nbsp;
played by Toby Leonard Moore. Well-dressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:11.920
soft-spoken, and eloquent, Wesley is likely one of&nbsp;
the last men you'd guess is a high-ranking member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:18.400
in a violent criminal organization. Yet in his&nbsp;
first scene, Wesley calmly sits on a park bench&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:24.480
next to a man who owes Fisk money and delivers&nbsp;
a monstrous threat against the man's daughter.

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:29.920
"Now, you see this man here? If we're&nbsp;
being honest with each other, Mr. Farnum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.720
I find his methods unpleasant."

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:39.680
It's because Wesley isn't the guy who works&nbsp;
with guns or knives that he's such a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:45.760
villain. He delivers his blows with a word or&nbsp;
a look. Wesley represents the more civilized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:50.560
side of Wilson Fisk, the part of himself&nbsp;
that the crime lord wants the world to see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:54.560
That's a big part of the reason&nbsp;
why when Karen Page kills Wesley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.560 --> 00:19:00.400
Fisk begins to do away with his photogenic facade&nbsp;
and deal with his enemies much more brazenly.

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:05.760
While it wasn't enough to save the&nbsp;
series, Iron Fist's second season is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:10.000
vast improvement on the first. One&nbsp;
of the things that makes season 2 so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:14.560
much better is Alice Eve's portrayal&nbsp;
of the mentally fractured Mary Walker.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:18.480
Every scene she's in makes you&nbsp;
worry about what she might do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:23.120
Employed by Joy Meachum first to follow&nbsp;
Danny Rand and then to get more hands on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:28.080
Walker is one of the most intimidating and&nbsp;
challenging foes Rand faces in the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:34.320
In spite of having no enhanced abilities, through&nbsp;
skill and careful planning, Mary wipes the floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:38.560
with Danny Rand in their first fight on&nbsp;
a subway platform in "Target: Iron Fist."

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:43.520
Madame Gao, played by Wai Ching Ho,&nbsp;
is one of the most dangerous people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:48.400
you'll meet in Marvel's Netflix shows. She&nbsp;
presents herself as a physically frail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:53.440
elderly woman in order to give enemies and&nbsp;
allies alike a false sense of security.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:58.480
She prefers to work from the shadows, letting her&nbsp;
actions speak for her and foregoing the noise and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:04.000
bravado of other bad guys. While she tends to be&nbsp;
a puppet master more than anything else, we learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:09.120
in The Defenders that Gao has physical abilities&nbsp;
that allow her to go toe to toe with Iron Fist.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:13.040
Not only is Madame Gao one of the&nbsp;
strongest pieces of connective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:18.640
tissue throughout Marvel's Netflix series,&nbsp;
she's perhaps the absolute last person you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:23.120
want to mess with. She may not lash&nbsp;
out in aggressive and bloody ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:27.440
but she'll give you that wicked smile&nbsp;
and just wait until the time is right.

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:32.640
In the first half of Luke Cage's first&nbsp;
season, Mariah Dillard wants Harlem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:37.680
see her as the legitimate face of the Stokes&nbsp;
family. So, she allows Cornell to lord over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:41.680
the criminal elements of the neighborhood.&nbsp;
Things change when he implies the sexual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:46.640
abuse Dillard endured at the hands of her uncle&nbsp;
was consensual. She kills Cornell in his club,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:51.920
and, supported by the manipulative Shades,&nbsp;
soon becomes the queen of crime in Harlem.

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:56.080
Alfre Woodard may have already had a&nbsp;
role in the MCU — as a grieving mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.080 --> 00:21:00.160
in 2016's Captain America: Civil War.&nbsp;
However, her performance in Luke Cage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:04.560
overrides any concerns a continuity&nbsp;
stickler might have. Through Woodard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:09.760
Mariah is a tortured villain, haunted by the&nbsp;
abuse she suffered from her criminal family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:15.760
Her rage and pain is bubbling at the surface&nbsp;
of every scene, and she's merciless enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:21.120
to leave no mystery as to how she could ever&nbsp;
be a threat to a man with bulletproof skin.

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:27.600
Few of Marvel's Netflix villains could leave such&nbsp;
a lasting impression as Jessica Jones' Kilgrave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:32.160
played by David Tennant. Gifted with the&nbsp;
ability to make anyone do what he tells them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:37.360
Kilgrave makes every character a potential&nbsp;
time bomb. Before the events of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:42.000
he uses his powers to force Jessica Jones&nbsp;
do a lot of things she doesn't want to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:47.120
including commit murder. The trauma she&nbsp;
suffers at his hands is what makes the hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:52.960
so miserable, and his presence is felt&nbsp;
throughout the show's amazing first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:57.200
even long before he and Jessica&nbsp;
finally reunite face to face.

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:02.400
David Tennant's performance is such that — even&nbsp;
though Jones kills him in the season 1 finale — he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:08.720
never fully leaves the series. Kilgrave leaves&nbsp;
scars in Jessica too deep to let him die for good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:14.560
We see this most clearly in season 2's "AKA&nbsp;
Three Lives and Counting," when Jones is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:19.440
plagued by visions of Kilgrave harassing her&nbsp;
and tempting her to lash out with violence.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:26.640
It's unthinkable to imagine someone other than&nbsp;
Vincent D'Onofrio in the role of Daredevil's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:32.080
important villain, Wilson Fisk. For the show's&nbsp;
three seasons, which is more than most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:37.760
Marvel Netflix series got and yet hardly feels&nbsp;
like enough, D'Onofrio's time as Fisk was one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:43.200
the best reasons to watch Daredevil. D'Onofrio&nbsp;
provides the perfect mix of intelligence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:49.680
civility, ruthlessness, rage, and turbulent&nbsp;
pain to bring us the man the comic books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:54.000
call the Kingpin. It's probably&nbsp;
not a coincidence that season 2 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:59.040
generally regarded as the weakest chapter&nbsp;
of Daredevil, since Fisk is mostly absent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:04.240
He only appears in the final moments of the&nbsp;
season's eighth episode, "Guilty as Sin."

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:09.440
Wilson Fisk embodies the incredible potential&nbsp;
of the stories that were being told on Netflix's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:16.160
Marvel shows. Fisk is no alien warlord or an&nbsp;
Asgardian god jealous of his brother. He's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:22.000
man of wealth, whose childhood scars drive&nbsp;
him to evil, and nothing about that sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:28.480
like make-believe. There's no reason why someone&nbsp;
like Wilson Fisk couldn't exist in the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:33.760
and that makes him more terrifying than&nbsp;
any other MCU villain you can think of.

